---
title: Ultimate Chinese Hamburger (Rou Jia Mo 肉夹馍)
description: A synthesized recipe combining the rich, caramel-braised pork belly of traditional Hongshaorou techniques with a laminated, crispy pan-to-oven Mo bread. Served "street food style" with fresh herbs and chilies.
image: https://thewoksoflife.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/rou-jia-mo-8.jpg
prep time: 1 hour 30 minutes
cook time: 3 hours 30 minutes
servings: 10
tags:
  - rou jia mo
  - street food
  - pork
  - sandwich
---

> Rou Jia Mo is incredibly forgiving. Every street vendor has their own secret spice blend, so don't worry if you are missing an optional spice. The secret to a great sandwich is getting that rich, mahogany color on the pork using the 'tangse' (caramel) technique, and achieving a flaky, laminated crust on the Mo bread!

== Pork Filling ==

If desired, soak the @pork belly{1%kg} (60% lean, 40% fat) in a #bowl{} of cold water for ~{1-2%hours} to remove impurities -- This mellows the pork flavor, but can be skipped if using high-quality or pre-cleaned pork. Drain well.

Make the tangse (caramel): Crush @rock sugar{30%g}. Heat a heavy #pot{} or wok over medium heat. Add @oil{1%tbsp} and the crushed sugar. Cook, stirring constantly, for about ~{5%minutes} until the sugar melts into a dark brown liquid -- Don't walk away! Caramel goes from perfect to burnt in seconds.

Stand back to avoid splattering, and carefully pour @warm water{500%ml} into the caramel. Bring it to a boil. 

Add the pork belly to the pot along with the remaining @rock sugar{30%g}, @Shaoxing wine{60%ml}, @dark soy sauce{3%tbsp}, @light soy sauce{2%tbsp}, @ginger{3%slices}, @leek{2%sections}, @Sichuan peppercorns{20}, @dried chilis{2}(deseeded), @star anise{3}, @cinnamon stick{1}, @bay leaves{3}, @black cardamom{1}(slightly crushed), and @whole cloves{10} [- Alternatively, use pork shoulder if you prefer less fat, but keep at least some pork belly for moisture. -] 

Add enough extra @water{} to ensure the pork is fully covered. Bring to a simmer, cover, and braise on low heat for ~{3%hours}. 

After three hours, uncover the pot, sprinkle @salt{1/2%tbsp} over the pork, and let it simmer uncovered for another ~{20%minutes}. Hold the pork in its braising liquid until ready to serve -- You can also strain and save the leftover braising liquid in the fridge to use as a master stock or spoon over noodles!

== Dough ==

While the pork is in its final hour of braising, start the dough. In a #stand mixer{} or large bowl, combine @all-purpose flour{500%g}, @active dry yeast{1.5%tsp}, @baking powder{1%tsp}, @oil{2%tbsp}, @warm water{220%ml}, and @salt{1/4%tsp}. 

Knead the mixture for ~{3-5%minutes} until a smooth dough forms. 

Form the dough into a ball, cover with a warm, damp cloth, and let it rise in a warm place for ~{40%minutes}, or until roughly doubled in size.

== Shaping Buns ==

Preheat your #oven{} to 250°C. If you have a cast iron pan, place it in the oven to preheat as well.

Deflate the dough and roll it into a rough log. Sprinkle the dough evenly with @baking soda{1/4%tsp} (to neutralize the yeast flavor), fold the dough over, and knead it briefly back into a log -- Err on the side of too little baking soda rather than too much to avoid a metallic taste.

Divide the dough into 10 equal balls (roughly 75g each).

To shape: Take one ball, form it into a pipe shape, and roll it out flat lengthwise. Smear a very light layer of @oil{} across the surface -- The oil here creates a laminated 'pocket' inside the bread, perfect for stuffing. Fold it in half lengthwise (hot-dog style), then roll it up tightly into a cylinder. Press it down flat with your palm, then use a rolling pin to roll it from the center-out into a 10 cm disc. Repeat for all buns.

== Cooking Buns ==

Carefully remove the hot #cast iron pan{} from the oven and place it on the stove over medium heat. 

Spritz the shaped buns lightly with water for extra crustiness. Place them in the dry pan. Toast on one side for ~{30%seconds}, flip, and toast on the other side for ~{20%seconds} until lightly browned.

Immediately transfer the pan (or place the buns on a baking sheet) back into the 250°C oven. Bake for ~{2%minutes} to finish the crust. 

== Assembly (Street Food Style) ==

Remove a portion of the pork belly from the braise and roughly chop it on a cutting board. 

Finely chop @cucumber{3%slices}, @green chili{3%slices}(like Anaheim), and @cilantro{3%sprigs}. 

Mix the chopped vegetables into the pork. Add a ladle of the braising liquid to moisten the mixture, and stir in @chili oil{1%tsp} if you like it spicy. Chop everything together until it reaches a cohesive, slightly mushy consistency.

Slice open a hot Mo bread, leaving the back intact to create a pocket. Stuff generously with the pork and vegetable mixture, add one final drizzle of braising sauce, and serve immediately.

